Human papillomavirus (HPV) infects epithelial cells of skin and mucous membranes; $>150$ types of HPV have been identified. These cause infections at different sites (see Box 7.9). Infections are transmitted by direct contact, which may be sexual, but the incubation time ranges from weeks to more than a year. HPV DNA is widely distributed on skin in the general population. Infection may be subclinical.  Human papillomavirus (HPV) infects epithelial cells of skin and mucous membranes; $>150$ types of HPV have been identified. These cause infections at different sites (see Box 7.9). Infections are transmitted by direct contact, which may be sexual, but the incubation time ranges from weeks to more than a year. HPV DNA is widely distributed on skin in the general population. Infection may be subclinical.
